---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/home-assistant/');

const webhookYaml = `# configuration.yaml — or an automation created in the UI
automation:
  - alias: Receive Hozz health data
    trigger:
      - platform: webhook
        webhook_id: hozz
        allowed_methods: [POST]
        local_only: true
    action:
      - variables:
          metrics: "{{ trigger.json.data.metrics }}"`;

const payload = `{
  "data": {
    "metrics": [
      {
        "name": "heart_rate",
        "units": "count/min",
        "data": [
          {
            "date": "2026-08-22T21:10:46.500Z",
            "qty": 62.5,
            "units": "count/min",
            "source": "Apple Watch"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "deletions": []
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>Metrics JSON</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Underneath</dt>
      <dd>An ordinary HTTPS POST</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Needs</dt>
      <dd>The phone to reach your HA</dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Hozz posts <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#metrics-json">Metrics JSON</a> to Home Assistant,
    either to a webhook trigger or to the REST API. It is an ordinary HTTPS endpoint underneath —
    the preset exists so you do not have to work out that it was one.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Decide: webhook or REST API</h3>
      <p>
        A <strong>webhook</strong> needs no token and is the simpler of the two. The{' '}
        <strong>REST API</strong> needs a long-lived access token, and suits you if you are already
        posting to it from other things.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>For a webhook, add the trigger</h3>
      <p>
        Create an automation with a webhook trigger and note its ID. The address Hozz shows as a
        placeholder is{' '}
        <code>http://homeassistant.local:8123/api/webhook/hozz</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>For the REST API, create a token</h3>
      <p>
        Under your Home Assistant profile, create a long-lived access token. In the Hozz
        destination's secret field, write <code>Bearer</code> followed by a space and the token
        itself — the field holds the whole header value, not just the credential.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Send a test</h3>
      <p>Home Assistant will show the request arriving. The probe contains no Health data.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Your phone has to be able to reach the address">
    <p>
      On a home network that means being on the same Wi-Fi. A <code>.local</code> address will not
      resolve over cellular, so if you want deliveries while you are out, use an address that is
      reachable from outside — and put it behind TLS if it is.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The webhook trigger</h2>

  <Code caption="Home Assistant automation" code={webhookYaml} />

  <p>
    <code>local_only: true</code> is worth keeping unless you deliberately want the webhook exposed.
    Hozz sends the batch as a single POST with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>What arrives</h2>

  <Code caption="POST body — Metrics JSON" code={payload} />

  <p>
    Points are grouped by metric name, in lowercase snake_case. Curated names are used where one
    exists — <code>HKQuantityTypeIdentifierStepCount</code> becomes <code>step_count</code>,{' '}
    <code>HKQuantityTypeIdentifierActiveEnergyBurned</code> becomes <code>active_energy</code> —
    and anything uncurated has its identifier prefix removed and the rest snake cased, so a type
    Hozz has never heard of still gets a stable, predictable name rather than being dropped.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#metrics-json">the delivery schema</a> for the full payload shape, including the <code>deletions</code> array.
  </p>

  <h2>Coming from Health Auto Export</h2>

  <p>
    If your automations are already keyed to Health Auto Export's field names, Hozz has an{' '}
    <strong>opt-in compatibility mode</strong> on this destination that emits those names instead.
    It is offered when the format is Metrics JSON.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/switching-from-health-auto-export/">Switching from Health Auto Export</a> covers what
    maps to what, and the three things it deliberately does not claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Telling it is working</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Send a test</strong> reports the status Home Assistant returned. Anything <code>2xx</code
      > is an acceptance.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Home Assistant, a webhook automation's trace shows the payload it received.
    </li>
    <li>
      On the phone, the destination shows <code>delivered</code> once everything staged has been
      accepted. <a href="/docs/background-sync/">The other six states</a> are all explained.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
